canonical_claim.statement:
As printed. p. 2: ‘We introduce a weaker, design-conditional variant of SUTVA that we call the “no unmodeled revealable variation assumption” (NURVA) (Condition 5.1) that requires stability only over the support of the design.’ p. 8: ‘Condition 5.1 (NURVA). $$g_i(\mathbf z)=g_i(\mathbf z')\implies y_i(\mathbf z)=y_i(\mathbf z')=\overline y_i(g_i(\mathbf z)),\ \forall\mathbf z,\mathbf z'\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z).$$ In contrast to the setting where we make no additional assumptions, under NURVA, each EPO is no longer composed of weighted averages of different possible raw potential outcomes, but instead consists of a unique and common raw potential outcome realized under any assignment $\mathbf z\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$ that results in the individual being exposed to $d$.’ ‘Second, we consider the more familiar assumption of Rubin’s SUTVA, which makes an assumption like NURVA, but does so across all feasible interventions.’ ‘For a given design, NURVA and SUTVA are observationally equivalent: NURVA embeds all observable implications of SUTVA, as all $\mathbf z\in\mathcal Z\setminus\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$ remain hidden under the experimental design. Hence, the gap is in scope.’
Formal content. For a design $\mathbf Z$ on $\mathcal Z$, exposure mappings $g_i$ and raw potential outcomes $y_i$:
(i) if $g_i(\mathbf z)=g_i(\mathbf z')\Rightarrow y_i(\mathbf z)=y_i(\mathbf z')$ for all $i$ and all $\mathbf z,\mathbf z'\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$, then for every $i$ and every $d$ with $\pi_i(d)>0$, $\overline y_i(d)=y_i(\mathbf z)$ for every $\mathbf z\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$ with $g_i(\mathbf z)=d$ (so the last equality of Condition 5.1 follows from the first), and $\tau_i(d,d')=y_i(\mathbf z)-y_i(\mathbf z')$ for any $\mathbf z,\mathbf z'\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$ with $g_i(\mathbf z)=d$, $g_i(\mathbf z')=d'$;
(ii) Condition 5.2 (SUTVA) on $\mathcal Z$ implies Condition 5.1 (NURVA) for every design whose support is contained in $\mathcal Z$;
(iii) observational equivalence: the joint distribution of the observed data $(\mathbf Z,\mathbf Y)$ depends on the raw potential outcomes only through their values on $\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$; and if NURVA holds for $(y,g,\mathbf Z)$, there are raw potential outcomes $y^*$ on $\mathcal Z$ that satisfy SUTVA and agree with $y$ on $\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$ (for instance $y_i^*(\mathbf z)$ equal to the common value of $y_i$ on $\{\mathbf z'\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z):g_i(\mathbf z')=g_i(\mathbf z)\}$ when that set is non-empty and to 0 otherwise). Hence the set of distributions of $(\mathbf Z,\mathbf Y)$ compatible with NURVA under the design equals the set compatible with SUTVA.
